4a:["$","p",null,{"children":"Training a perceptron involves finding the optimal weights and bias that minimize the error on the training data. This process can be understood as moving a decision boundary in the feature space until it correctly classifies the training examples."}]
4b:["$","p",null,{"children":"In the case of a 2D perceptron, the decision boundary can be expressed as a line:"}]
4c:["$","$L64",null,{"children":["$","$L65",null,{"children":["$","code",null,{"children":"Ax + By + C = 0\n"}]}]}]
4d:["$","p",null,{"children":["The goal is to find the coefficients ",["$","code",null,{"children":"A"}]," and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"B"}]," that best separate the data. Training starts with a random decision boundary, and the algorithm iteratively adjusts the coefficients by comparing the predicted output with the actual data points. This adjustment continues until the boundary correctly classifies all points or until a specified number of iterations (epochs) is reached."]}]
4e:["$","h3",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"moving-the-line--transformations","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#moving-the-line--transformations","className":"peer","children":"Moving the Line — Transformations:"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
4f:["$","ul",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"A"}],": Adjusts the line's slope about the x-axis."]}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"B"}],": Adjusts the line's slope about the y-axis."]}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"C"}],": Moves the line parallel to its original position."]}],"\n"]}]
50:["$","p",null,{"children":["To visualize how these transformations work, you can use tools like ",["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"https://www.desmos.com/","children":"Desmos"}]," to see how adjusting these coefficients shifts the decision boundary in real-time."]}]
51:["$","h3",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"perceptron-trick-explained","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#perceptron-trick-explained","className":"peer","children":"Perceptron Trick Explained"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
52:["$","p",null,{"children":"Imagine you're teaching a computer program to distinguish between two categories, say cats and dogs in pictures. Initially, the program makes random guesses, which are often incorrect. The Perceptron Trick is a method that helps the program learn from its mistakes and improve its predictions."}]
53:["$","p",null,{"children":"Here's how it works:"}]
54:["$","ol",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"The program makes a prediction."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"If the prediction is correct, nothing changes."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"If the prediction is wrong, the program adjusts its weights slightly in the direction that would correct the error."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"This process repeats for many examples, and over time, the program's predictions improve."}],"\n"]}]
55:["$","p",null,{"children":"So, the Perceptron Trick is a way for the computer to learn and get better at classifying things, much like how we learn from our mistakes."}]
56:["$","h3",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"main-algorithm","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#main-algorithm","className":"peer","children":"Main Algorithm"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
57:["$","$L64",null,{"children":["$","$L65",null,{"children":["$","code",null,{"className":"language-python","children":"import numpy as np\n\ndef perceptron_training(X, y, learning_rate=0.01, epochs=100):\n    # Initialize weights and bias\n    W = np.zeros(X.shape[1])  # Assuming X is a NumPy array\n    b = 0\n\n    for epoch in range(epochs):\n        no_errors = True\n\n        for i in range(len(X)):\n            # Calculate weighted sum\n            weighted_sum = np.dot(W, X[i]) + b\n            \n            # Predict class label (Step function)\n            y_pred = 1 if weighted_sum > 0 else 0\n            \n            # Compute the error\n            error = y[i] - y_pred\n            \n            # Update weights and bias if misclassified\n            if error != 0:\n                W += learning_rate * error * X[i]\n                b += learning_rate * error\n                no_errors = False\n\n        # Stop early if no misclassification in the entire epoch\n        if no_errors:\n            break\n\n    return W, b\n"}]}]}]
58:["$","h3",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"during-training","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#during-training","className":"peer","children":"During Training"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
59:["$","ul",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"If a point belonging to the negative class is predicted as positive, the weights are adjusted to move the decision boundary upwards."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"If a point belonging to the positive class is misclassified as negative, the weights are adjusted to move the boundary downwards."}],"\n"]}]
5a:["$","p",null,{"children":"Instead of testing two conditions for updating the weights, a simplified rule is often used:"}]
5b:["$","h3",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"perceptron-weight-update-rule","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#perceptron-weight-update-rule","className":"peer","children":"Perceptron Weight Update Rule"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
5c:["$","p",null,{"children":"For misclassified points:"}]
5d:["$","ul",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":["\n",["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"False Negative (Actual = 1, Predicted = 0)"}],":"]}],"\n",["$","ul",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"The model underestimates the class label."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"To correct this, we increase the weights by adding a small portion of the input value (scaled by the learning rate)."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Update Rule"}],":","\n",["$","$L64",null,{"children":["$","$L65",null,{"children":["$","code",null,{"children":"New weight = Old weight + (Learning rate × Input value)\n"}]}]}],"\n"]}],"\n"]}],"\n"]}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":["\n",["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"False Positive (Actual = 0, Predicted = 1)"}],":"]}],"\n",["$","ul",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"The model overestimates the class label."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":"To fix this, we decrease the weights by subtracting a small portion of the input value."}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Update Rule"}],":","\n",["$","$L64",null,{"children":["$","$L65",null,{"children":["$","code",null,{"children":"New weight = Old weight − (Learning rate × Input value)\n"}]}]}],"\n"]}],"\n"]}],"\n"]}],"\n"]}]
5e:["$","p",null,{"children":"This process repeats across multiple training cycles (epochs) until the model classifies all training points correctly or reaches a stopping condition."}]
5f:["$","h2",null,{"className":"flex scroll-m-28 flex-row items-center gap-2","id":"conclusion","children":[["$","a",null,{"data-card":"","href":"#conclusion","className":"peer","children":"Conclusion"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide size-3.5 shrink-0 text-fd-muted-foreground opacity-0 transition-opacity peer-hover:opacity-100","aria-label":"Link to section","children":[[["$","path","1cjeqo",{"d":"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"}],["$","path","19qd67",{"d":"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"}]],"$undefined"]}]]}]
60:["$","p",null,{"children":"The perceptron is a powerful yet simple model that laid the groundwork for more sophisticated neural networks. Understanding the Perceptron Trick and the geometric intuition behind perceptrons will provide you with a solid foundation in machine learning. While perceptrons have their limitations, particularly with non-linear data, they remain a crucial concept in the field."}]
